A||N||11||kornholio||Bad gas mileage...continued||||9||
Z||000000||kornholio||12-01-1999||05:58 PM||||I bought my 86 SE auto in June and I have been getting REALLY bad gas mileage.  Something like 15-16 mpg (city driving).  On a full tank, I can get about 140-150 miles.  Here are the things that I have done since buying the car (some of these things were replaced due to failure)...<P>replaced:<BR>EGR tube<BR>EGR valve<BR>EGR solenoid<BR>spark plugs + wires<BR>distributor cap + rotor<BR>TPS<BR>air filter<BR>fuel filter<BR>battery<BR>alternator<BR>catalytic convertor<BR>thermostat<BR>Also had an oil and transmission fluid change.<P>I should get my timing checked.  How much would this effect gas mileage?<P>Does anyone know what is causing such bad gas mileage?  Can you think of anything that I should check/replace?  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 12-01-1999).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||ChadMan||12-02-1999||10:17 AM||||A bad oxygen sensor will throw your MPG in the toilet.  A bad MAP sensor can do it too.  Both can cause the car to run too rich.  I'd say the oxygen sensor is the more likely culprit.  (It's cheaper, too.)||||reg||3
Z||000002||kornholio||12-02-1999||04:17 PM||||My oxygen sensor and MAP sensor are functioning.  I don't get any error codes.  How about bad compression?  I haven't had that tested yet.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||12-02-1999||04:29 PM||||How about the ECM itself?  Generaly speaking from my limited experience, if the computer itself is out of whack, it's not going to tell you cause it doesn't know!  Granted, they don't go bad very often, but with all the other stuff you have changed, it seems like as likely a candidate as anything else in the system and it DOES control all the rest of it.  Just another thought to ponder...||||reg||5
Z||000004||kornholio||12-02-1999||04:36 PM||||I think that my ECM is OK.  It used to give me code 32 before I changed the EGR solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||12-02-1999||04:58 PM||||If the timing is off it could have a very negative effect on mileage.  Also, the oxygen sensor can be "worn out" enough to drag the mileage down without setting any trouble codes in the computer.||||reg||1
Z||000006||kornholio||12-02-1999||05:03 PM||||Is there a way to tell whether if your timing is WAY off, besides using a timing gun?  Is there a certain noise the engine makes?  I just want to know if it is worth while to pay someone to check my timing.  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 12-02-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-02-1999||05:06 PM||||If your timing is WAY off the engine won't run.  A timing light is really the only way to know if the timing is very close.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||12-02-1999||07:46 PM||||Two sensors thst can cause this come to mind. The o2 sensor or the coolant temperature sensor out of range.||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||12-02-1999||07:46 PM||||Two sensors that can cause this come to mind. The o2 sensor or the coolant temperature sensor out of range.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mark||12-02-1999||08:28 PM||||I have recently been getting about 17 or 18 mpg (city driving) in my '86 GT (std) with &lt; 36K miles. I wasn't impressed with this mileage either, so I installed a fresh O2 sensor, 195 degree thermostat, checked the coolant temperature to the ECM using a scan tool (about 200 F as I recall), checked the timing (right on the money), and checked the TPS signal with a scan tool(nice and stable, and proportional to throttle position).  Additionally, the car passes PA enhanced emission testing with flying colors and generally runs very strong. <P>I have noticed two odd performance characteristics: 1)the engine fast idles (at about 2000 rpm) for what seems like an unnecessarily long time (maybe 4 or 5 minutes) after a cold start, then settles down to about 950, and 2) when decelerating, I sometimes get a whif of what seems like rich exhaust.  Maybe an excessively long cold start injector spray period is  contributing to the overall low average. Does anyone know of any specifications for this, or ideas of what could cause a rich mixture during deceleration?  I'm beginning to believe that 17 or 18 mpg (city)is normal.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tim||12-02-1999||10:38 PM||||I'm get bad MPG, right now about 17-18 MPG.  I was getting worst!  I installed an A/F ratio gauge, the oxygen sensor was showing  lean.  The ECM would richen to try to make it right.  I tried a couple different used OS and got different results.  I bought a new heated OS(Bosch 13077) and I went from 12 miles per gallon to 17-18.  The heated sensor will enter closed-loop sooner.  I never had a code set and when I checked the old sensor with a DVM I was within range and getting cross-counts.  A scanner showed the old OS okay.  <p>[This message has been edited by Tim (edited 12-02-1999).]||||reg||1
